  "name": "CHOLINESTERASE (BUTRYL)",
  "code": "1056",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"167\" data-end=\"321\"><strong data-start=\"167\" data-end=\"209\">Cholinesterase (Butyrylcholinesterase)</strong> is an enzyme produced by the liver that hydrolyzes certain choline-based esters. This test is commonly used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"323\" data-end=\"754\">\n<li data-start=\"323\" data-end=\"414\">\n<p data-start=\"325\" data-end=\"414\"><strong data-start=\"325\" data-end=\"387\">Detect exposure to organophosphate or carbamate pesticides</strong> (which inhibit the enzyme)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"415\" data-end=\"482\">\n<p data-start=\"417\" data-end=\"482\"><strong data-start=\"417\" data-end=\"444\">Evaluate liver function</strong>, as levels drop in hepatic impairment</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"483\" data-end=\"661\">\n<p data-start=\"485\" data-end=\"661\"><strong data-start=\"485\" data-end=\"514\">Identify genetic variants</strong> causing atypical cholinesterase activity, important before administering drugs like <strong data-start=\"599\" data-end=\"618\">succinylcholine</strong> (a muscle relaxant used during anesthesia)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"662\" data-end=\"754\">\n<p data-start=\"664\" data-end=\"754\"><strong data-start=\"664\" data-end=\"684\">Monitor recovery</strong> in individuals previously exposed to cholinesterase-inhibiting agents</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Nephrotic syndrome</strong></td>\n<td>Increased enzyme due to protein changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Diabetes mellitus</strong></td>\n<td>Mild elevations may occur</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hyperlipidemia</strong></td>\n<td>Associated with increased plasma cholinesterase activity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Certain cancers</strong></td>\n<td>Some solid tumors can elevate cholinesterase levels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Recovery from liver disease or pesticide exposure</strong></td>\n<td>Enzyme levels can rebound above normal during convalescence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Elevated levels are generally not of clinical concern unless monitored for trends post-exposure or liver dysfunction.</p>",
